Scott 1976, Theorem 4.6, the inverse-limit half. The order isomorphism between the fixed points of the least fixed point of a Scott-continuous, strictness-preserving, retract-monotone retract functor and the inverse limit of its Kleene stages sends a fixed point to the sequence of its stage projections and a coherent sequence to the union of its coordinates. The union of the stage projections of a fixed point recovers the fixed point, by application of a union, the least-fixed-point decomposition, and the fixed-point equation. Each coordinate of a coherent sequence is recovered by projecting the union at that stage. One fixed point lies below another exactly when every stage projection does, because each fixed point equals the union of its stage projections and each stage projection is monotone.
